      "rev_n_1_ae6484_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_ae6484_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_ae6484",
        "thought_text": "Calculate the total sum of all weights and then iterate through possible values of T to find the division that minimizes the absolute difference between S_1 and S_2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500220.904325
      },
      "rev_n_2_47c7f2_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_47c7f2_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_47c7f2",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to keep track of all possible sums that can be formed using the first T weights and then determine the closest sum to half of the total sum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500220.9043386
      },
      "rev_n_3_5b0bfe_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_5b0bfe_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_5b0bfe",
        "thought_text": "Sort the weights and use a greedy algorithm to assign weights to the two groups, starting with the largest weights, to minimize the difference between S_1 and S_2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500220.9043467
      },
      "rev_n_2_47c7f2_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_47c7f2_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_47c7f2",
        "thought_text": "The issue arises because the dynamic programming approach used here does not correctly handle the partitioning of weights into two groups. Instead, we should use a different approach that considers all possible ways to split the weights into two groups and calculates the minimum absolute difference between their sums. This can be achieved using a recursive function with memoization to avoid redundant calculations.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_47c7f2_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_bcc3aee7ad",
        "created_at": 1794500236.1238997
      },
      "rev_n_3_5b0bfe_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_5b0bfe_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_5b0bfe",
        "thought_text": "The greedy approach of assigning the largest weights first does not guarantee the minimum possible absolute difference between the sums of the two groups. Instead, we should consider using dynamic programming to explore all possible ways to partition the weights into two groups and find the one with the smallest absolute difference. This involves creating a DP table where `dp[i][j]` represents whether it's possible to achieve a sum of `j` using the first `i` weights. We then iterate through all possible partitions and calculate the absolute difference between the sums of the two groups, keeping track of the minimum difference found.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_5b0bfe_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_2ec288148f",
        "created_at": 1794500252.2820144
      },
      "rev_n_4_e6ab02_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_e6ab02_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_e6ab02",
        "thought_text": "Calculate the total sum of all weights and then iterate through possible values of T to find the division that minimizes the absolute difference between S_1 and S_2.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8837731912261671124",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500265.1886258
      },
      "rev_n_5_c1d663_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_c1d663_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_c1d663",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to keep track of all possible sums that can be formed using the first T weights and compare them with the remaining weights to minimize the absolute difference.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8837731912261671124",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794500265.1886413
      },
      "rev_n_6_2755d0_0": {
